5.5 Summary of quantifiers

Universal quantifier
\[
\begin{aligned}
\langle universal\rangle & ::= \forall \langle \text{variable}\rangle . \langle\text{Predicate}\rangle
&  (L_{\forall})
\\[5pt]
& \begin{array}{rl} \Gamma, c & \vdash P(c) \\ \hline \Gamma & \vdash \forall x.P(x)\end{array}  
& (I_{\forall})\\[5pt]
& \begin{array}{rl} \Gamma & \vdash \forall x.P(x)\\ \Gamma & \vdash c \\ \hline \Gamma & \vdash P(c)\end{array}
& (E_{\forall})\\
\end{aligned}
\]


Existential quantifier.
\[
\begin{aligned}
\langle existential\rangle & ::= \exists \langle \text{variable}\rangle . \langle\text{Predicate}\rangle
&  (L_{\exists})
\\[5pt]
& \begin{array}{rl} \Gamma & \vdash P(c) \\ \hline \Gamma & \vdash \exists x.P(x)\end{array}  
& (I_{\exists})\\[5pt]
& \begin{array}{rl} \Gamma & \vdash \exists x.P(x)\\ \Gamma, c & \vdash P(c) \\ \hline \Gamma & \vdash Q\end{array}
& (E_{\exists})\\
\end{aligned}
\]

12.3 Calculus with Sums, Products, and Powers Copy
Now it is time to get some useful calculation facts on integral and derivatives. While these are often explained with pictures of areas, volumes, and slopes. We can apply these wherever we can measure. So these are in fact properties we can use even when our concepts of area and slope are merely metaphorical. This is part of the appeal of a calculus: it applies in so many different contexts.

